1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,994
A water tank fills from the bottom up.

2
00:00:02,994 --> 00:00:06,718
In a bus, strangers take empty
seats before anyone shares.

3
00:00:06,718 --> 00:00:10,056
And no two passengers can
hold the same seat number.

4
00:00:10,456 --> 00:00:12,101
Three everyday habits.

5
00:00:12,101 --> 00:00:15,134
Electrons follow the
very same three rules.

6
00:00:15,584 --> 00:00:17,257
Diya sees it at college.

7
00:00:17,257 --> 00:00:21,433
In the reading room, every new
student takes an empty table first.

8
00:00:21,433 --> 00:00:24,472
Sharing starts only when
every table has someone.

9
00:00:24,822 --> 00:00:27,331
Electrons do the same inside an atom.

10
00:00:27,331 --> 00:00:30,349
But who decides which
orbital they fill first?

11
00:00:30,849 --> 00:00:35,314
By the end of this video, you will
fill any atom, one electron at a time,

12
00:00:35,314 --> 00:00:36,835
with three simple rules.

13
00:00:37,485 --> 00:00:39,101
First, the energy ladder.

14
00:00:39,451 --> 00:00:44,001
In a hydrogen atom, the energy
of an orbital depends only on n.

15
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:48,445
But in atoms with many electrons,
it depends on both n and l.

16
00:00:48,845 --> 00:00:53,104
Inner electrons shield the outer
ones from the pull of the nucleus.

17
00:00:53,104 --> 00:00:56,545
So subshells of the same
shell get different energies.

18
00:00:56,545 --> 00:00:58,586
Two s sits lower than two p.

19
00:00:59,186 --> 00:01:01,052
So which subshell is lower?

20
00:01:01,052 --> 00:01:02,731
Use the n plus l rule.

21
00:01:03,081 --> 00:01:05,004
Add n and l for each subshell.

22
00:01:05,004 --> 00:01:07,513
The lower the sum, the lower the energy.

23
00:01:07,913 --> 00:01:10,717
Four s has n plus l equal to four.

24
00:01:10,717 --> 00:01:12,227
Three d has five.

25
00:01:12,227 --> 00:01:14,727
So four s fills before three d.

26
00:01:15,127 --> 00:01:19,974
If two subshells have the same sum,
the one with lower n comes first.

27
00:01:19,974 --> 00:01:22,512
Three d and four p both have five.

28
00:01:22,512 --> 00:01:24,016
Three d goes first.

29
00:01:24,416 --> 00:01:29,911
This gives the order: one s, two s,
two p, three s, three p, four s, three

30
00:01:29,911 --> 00:01:32,402
d, four p, five s, and so on.

31
00:01:33,052 --> 00:01:34,579
Now the three rules.

32
00:01:34,579 --> 00:01:36,833
Rule one, the Aufbau principle.

33
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:39,149
Aufbau is German for building up.

34
00:01:39,149 --> 00:01:43,402
Orbitals are filled in order of
increasing energy, lowest first.

35
00:01:43,802 --> 00:01:46,605
Rule two, the Pauli exclusion principle.

36
00:01:46,605 --> 00:01:51,621
No two electrons in an atom can have
the same set of four quantum numbers.

37
00:01:52,021 --> 00:01:56,526
So one orbital holds at most two
electrons, and they must have opposite

38
00:01:56,526 --> 00:01:57,426
spins.

39
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:58,588
One up, one down.

40
00:01:58,988 --> 00:02:02,417
Rule three, Hund's rule
of maximum multiplicity.

41
00:02:02,417 --> 00:02:07,758
In a p, d or f subshell, electrons do
not pair up until every orbital has

42
00:02:07,758 --> 00:02:08,880
one electron.

43
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:13,383
And those single electrons spin
the same way, with parallel spins.

44
00:02:14,034 --> 00:02:17,077
Let's fill nitrogen on our molecule bench.

45
00:02:17,077 --> 00:02:19,247
Nitrogen has seven electrons.

46
00:02:19,647 --> 00:02:23,575
Electrons one and two go into
one s, one up and one down.

47
00:02:23,975 --> 00:02:25,700
Three and four fill two s.

48
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:27,109
Now two p.

49
00:02:27,109 --> 00:02:29,615
Electron five takes the first box.

50
00:02:29,615 --> 00:02:31,687
Six and seven do not pair.

51
00:02:31,687 --> 00:02:35,201
Each takes an empty box,
spinning the same way.

52
00:02:35,601 --> 00:02:38,582
Nitrogen ends with three
unpaired electrons.

53
00:02:38,582 --> 00:02:42,678
Add one more for oxygen, and
only now does pairing begin.

54
00:02:43,328 --> 00:02:46,782
Look at two drawings of the
two p electrons of carbon.

55
00:02:46,782 --> 00:02:48,658
In one, both share a box.

56
00:02:48,658 --> 00:02:51,414
In the other, they sit in separate boxes.

57
00:02:51,814 --> 00:02:52,924
Pause and think.

58
00:02:52,924 --> 00:02:54,223
Which one is right?

59
00:02:57,423 --> 00:02:59,424
Separate boxes, same spin.

60
00:02:59,424 --> 00:03:00,948
That is Hund's rule.

61
00:03:00,948 --> 00:03:04,378
Pairing waits until every
p box has one electron.

62
00:03:05,028 --> 00:03:08,600
Now let's order three
subshells, one step at a time.

63
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:10,865
Four p, five s, and four d.

64
00:03:11,215 --> 00:03:12,120
Step one.

65
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:13,433
Find n plus l.

66
00:03:13,433 --> 00:03:15,937
Four p is four plus one, five.

67
00:03:15,937 --> 00:03:18,497
Five s is five plus zero, five.

68
00:03:18,497 --> 00:03:20,946
Four d is four plus two, six.

69
00:03:21,696 --> 00:03:22,596
Step two.

70
00:03:22,537 --> 00:03:24,487
The lowest sum fills first.

71
00:03:24,487 --> 00:03:27,033
Four d has six, so it comes last.

72
00:03:27,783 --> 00:03:28,833
Step three.

73
00:03:28,833 --> 00:03:31,380
Four p and five s tie at five.

74
00:03:31,380 --> 00:03:34,977
Lower n wins, so four
p comes before five s.

75
00:03:35,727 --> 00:03:36,655
Step four.

76
00:03:36,655 --> 00:03:40,289
The order is four p,
then five s, then four d.

77
00:03:41,039 --> 00:03:42,105
Remember this.

78
00:03:42,105 --> 00:03:46,557
Lowest energy first, two opposite
spins per box, and spread out before

79
00:03:46,557 --> 00:03:47,457
you pair.

80
00:03:48,073 --> 00:03:49,809
Now, one worked example.

81
00:03:50,109 --> 00:03:52,199
Phosphorus has fifteen electrons.

82
00:03:52,199 --> 00:03:54,856
Fill them, and count the unpaired ones.

83
00:03:55,356 --> 00:03:56,787
One s takes two.

84
00:03:56,787 --> 00:03:58,218
Two s takes two.

85
00:03:58,218 --> 00:03:59,649
Two p takes six.

86
00:03:59,649 --> 00:04:01,313
That is ten so far.

87
00:04:02,063 --> 00:04:03,690
Three s takes two more.

88
00:04:03,690 --> 00:04:05,069
Now we have twelve.

89
00:04:05,819 --> 00:04:08,278
Three electrons are left for three p.

90
00:04:08,278 --> 00:04:12,404
By Hund's rule, one goes in each
box, all with the same spin.

91
00:04:13,154 --> 00:04:16,139
So phosphorus has three
unpaired electrons.

92
00:04:16,789 --> 00:04:18,255
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:18,605 --> 00:04:22,754
Many students draw two electrons
in one box, both pointing up.

94
00:04:23,154 --> 00:04:25,133
That breaks the Pauli principle.

95
00:04:25,133 --> 00:04:27,921
Both would have the same
four quantum numbers.

96
00:04:27,921 --> 00:04:30,774
In one box, one must
point up and one down.

97
00:04:31,424 --> 00:04:32,992
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:32,992 --> 00:04:37,655
In JEE Main, usually as arranging
orbitals in order of energy with the

99
00:04:37,655 --> 00:04:38,908
n plus l rule.

100
00:04:39,508 --> 00:04:40,769
Exam level now.

101
00:04:40,769 --> 00:04:45,678
What is the largest number of electrons
that can go into all the subshells

102
00:04:45,678 --> 00:04:47,892
with n plus l equal to five?

103
00:04:47,892 --> 00:04:49,207
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:54,607 --> 00:04:55,777
The answer is C.

105
00:04:55,777 --> 00:04:56,677
Eighteen.

106
00:04:56,966 --> 00:05:00,647
n plus l is five for three
d, four p and five s.

107
00:05:00,647 --> 00:05:04,317
There is no two f, because
l must be less than n.

108
00:05:04,817 --> 00:05:09,055
Three d holds ten, four p holds
six, and five s holds two.

109
00:05:09,555 --> 00:05:12,059
Ten plus six plus two is eighteen.

110
00:05:12,059 --> 00:05:14,288
Option B counted only three d.

111
00:05:14,888 --> 00:05:16,345
Here is your recap.

112
00:05:16,345 --> 00:05:17,842
Screenshot this card.

113
00:05:21,042 --> 00:05:23,878
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

114
00:05:24,278 --> 00:05:25,178
One.

115
00:05:24,748 --> 00:05:27,831
Which fills first, four
s or three d, and why?

116
00:05:28,631 --> 00:05:29,531
Two.

117
00:05:29,226 --> 00:05:32,643
Why can one orbital
hold only two electrons?

118
00:05:33,443 --> 00:05:34,343
Three.

119
00:05:34,093 --> 00:05:37,311
How many unpaired electrons
does nitrogen have?

120
00:05:38,211 --> 00:05:41,812
Next time: why does chromium
break the filling order?

121
00:05:41,812 --> 00:05:44,517
We find out in electronic configurations.
